{"type":"message","id":"73d5301c","parentId":"cdc48ec5","timestamp":"2026-05-22T10:28:59.138Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_DMwdbA9wARfz2yPuPVT7QfLM","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-101746/research/validated.json"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8M5zsq1R8ClTmKn8skKeRheW","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/research.json"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_IiYs4njqByR0BKDCBjaCINTO","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-writer/COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":6085,"output":149,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":6234,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779445734779,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiHPcamXKT1rz1mH8i1PKyINH6bMH"}}
{"type":"message","id":"f72363b5","parentId":"73d5301c","timestamp":"2026-05-22T10:28:59.187Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_DMwdbA9wARfz2yPuPVT7QfLM","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"polymarket-polygon-exploit-2026-05-22\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Polymarket rewards-system exploit on Polygon drains over $520,000 while core user funds remain safe\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Polymarket exploit\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"ZachXBT flags $520K Polymarket exploit on Polygon, team says funds are safe\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Polygon\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"polygon-ecosystem-token\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"BeInCrypto\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/22/zachxbt-flags-usd520k-polymarket-exploit-on-polygon-team-says-funds-are-safe\",\n    \"https://beincrypto.com/polymarket-exploit-with-520000-in-losses/\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Blockchain investigator ZachXBT said more than $520,000 was drained from two Polymarket-related smart contracts on Polygon.\",\n    \"CoinDesk reported the affected contract addresses as 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5, with funds allegedly sent to attacker address 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\",\n    \"Polymarket developers said the incident was a private-key compromise tied to an internal operations wallet used for rewards payouts, not a broader failure of core contracts or market resolution systems.\",\n    \"Polygon Labs CTO Mudit Gupta said Polymarket contracts are safe, user funds are safe, and the compromised component appeared to be the market initializer rather than the contracts themselves.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto identified the affected component as Polymarket\\u2019s UMA CTF Adapter, which connects prediction-market settlement to UMA\\u2019s Optimistic Oracle.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto, citing Bubblemaps, said the exploiter dispersed the stolen proceeds across 15 addresses, a pattern often associated with early-stage laundering attempts.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto added broader context that May had already seen five hacks in the prior week, 19 incidents for the month, and roughly $38.2 million in cumulative exploit losses according to DeFiLlama data.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"# ZachXBT flags $520K Polymarket exploit on Polygon, team says funds are safe\\n\\n## Blockchain investigator ZachXBT has highlighted a suspected security breach involving Polymarket, the world\\u2019s largest decentralized prediction market platform.\\n\\n#### What to know:\\n\\n- Blockchain sleuth ZachXBT flagged a suspected security breach at Polymarket in which more than $520,000 was drained from two smart contracts on the Polygon blockchain.\\n- Polymarket developers said the incident stemmed from a private key compromise of an internal operations wallet tied to its rewards payout system, stressing that user funds and market resolutions remain safe.\\n\\nBlockchain investigator ZachXBT has highlighted a suspected security breach involving Polymarket, the world\\u2019s largest decentralized prediction market platform.\\n\\nOver $520,000 was reportedly drained from two smart contracts on the Polygon blockchain, according to on-chain data shared by ZachXBT. The affected addresses are 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5, with funds allegedly sent to attacker address 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\\n\\nPolymarket developers said the company is aware of reports tied to its rewards payout system in a post on X. The team emphasized that user funds and market resolutions remain safe, describing the issue as a private key compromise of an internal operations wallet rather than a broader smart contract exploit or core infrastructure breach. Further updates are expected.\\n\\nPolygon Labs' CTO Mudit Gupta also commented on the incident, stating:\\n\\n\\u201cPolymarket contracts are safe. User funds are safe. Looks like their market initializer was compromised. No impact to the users or the contracts.\\u201d\\n\\nPolymarket has not yet issued an official statement from its main X account. CoinDesk has reached out to the company for additional comment. The incident comes amid heightened scrutiny of decentralized finance platforms.\\n\\n**On-chain investigator ZachXBT issued a community alert flagging an apparent exploit of Polymarket\\u2019s UMA CTF Adapter.**\\n\\nThe UMA CTF Adapter is the smart contract that lets Polymarket\\u2019s prediction markets settle using UMA\\u2019s Optimistic Oracle\\n\\nAccording to the alert, attackers have drained more than $520,000 so far. ZachXBT highlighted that the attacker\\u2019s address was 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\\n\\nAccording to Bubblemaps, the exploiter has since dispersed the proceeds across 15 addresses. This pattern is typically seen in the early stages of an on-chain laundering attempt.\\n\\n*Follow us on X** to get the latest news as it happens*\\n\\nThe situation is developing, and the full scope of the incident, as well as any response from Polymarket, has yet to be confirmed. BeInCrypto has contacted Polymarket for comment on the reported exploit and will update this story as more information becomes available.\\n\\nThe Polymarket incident lands amid a broader spike in DeFi exploits this month. May has already produced five separate hacks in the past week alone, pushing the monthly tally to 19, according to DeFiLlama data. Cumulative losses across those incidents have reached roughly $38.2 million.\\n\\n*Subscribe to our YouTube** channel to watch leaders and journalists provide expert insights*\\n\\nFeed metadata and corroborating details:\\n- CoinDesk RSS publication time: 2026-05-22T09:39:55+00:00. Headline: ZachXBT flags $520K Polymarket exploit on Polygon, team says funds are safe. Summary: Blockchain investigator ZachXBT has highlighted a suspected security breach involving Polymarket, the world\\u2019s largest decentralized prediction market platform.\\n- BeInCrypto RSS publication time: 2026-05-22T08:32:43+00:00. Headline: ZachXBT Flags Polymarket Smart-Contract Exploit With $520,000 in Losses. Summary: On-chain investigator ZachXBT issued a community alert flagging an apparent exploit of Polymarket\\u2019s UMA CTF Adapter. The UMA CTF Adapter is the smart contract that lets Polymarket\\u2019s prediction markets settle using UMA\\u2019s Optimistic Oracle.\\n- CoinDesk article references two affected addresses: 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5. The alleged attacker address is 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\\n- BeInCrypto reports Bubblemaps said the exploiter dispersed proceeds across 15 addresses, a pattern it described as typical of an early-stage laundering attempt.\\n- CoinDesk embedded/linked X posts: https://x.com/PolymarketDevs/status/2057755280641814769 and https://x.com/Mudit__Gupta/status/2057749582575202570.\\n- URL validation results before inclusion: CoinDesk 200 OK; BeInCrypto returned 403 to direct curl, but its RSS entry and trafilatura extraction still produced article text, title-aligned facts, and publication timestamp.\\n\",\n  \"tweet_quotes\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://x.com/PolymarketDevs/status/2057755280641814769\",\n      \"author\": \"@PolymarketDevs\",\n      \"text\": \"We\\u2019re aware of the security reports linked to rewards payout. User funds and market resolution are safe. \\n\\nFindings point to a private key compromise of a wallet used for internal top-up operations, not contracts or core infrastructure.\\n\\nMore updates to follow.\",\n      \"verified\": true,\n      \"source_article_url\": \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/22/zachxbt-flags-usd520k-polymarket-exploit-on-polygon-team-says-funds-are-safe\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://x.com/Mudit__Gupta/status/2057749582575202570\",\n      \"author\": \"@Mudit__Gupta\",\n      \"text\": \"Polymarket contracts are safe. User funds are safe.\\n\\nLooks like their market initializer was compromised. No impact to the users or the contracts.\",\n      \"verified\": true,\n      \"source_article_url\": \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/22/zachxbt-flags-usd520k-polymarket-exploit-on-polygon-team-says-funds-are-safe\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779445739187}}
{"type":"message","id":"fa1ce8b7","parentId":"f72363b5","timestamp":"2026-05-22T10:28:59.188Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_8M5zsq1R8ClTmKn8skKeRheW","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"polymarket-polygon-exploit-2026-05-22\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Polymarket rewards-system exploit on Polygon drains over $520,000 while core user funds remain safe\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Polymarket exploit\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"ZachXBT flags $520K Polymarket exploit on Polygon, team says funds are safe\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Polygon\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"polygon-ecosystem-token\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"BeInCrypto\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/22/zachxbt-flags-usd520k-polymarket-exploit-on-polygon-team-says-funds-are-safe\",\n    \"https://beincrypto.com/polymarket-exploit-with-520000-in-losses/\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Blockchain investigator ZachXBT said more than $520,000 was drained from two Polymarket-related smart contracts on Polygon.\",\n    \"CoinDesk reported the affected contract addresses as 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5, with funds allegedly sent to attacker address 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\",\n    \"Polymarket developers said the incident was a private-key compromise tied to an internal operations wallet used for rewards payouts, not a broader failure of core contracts or market resolution systems.\",\n    \"Polygon Labs CTO Mudit Gupta said Polymarket contracts are safe, user funds are safe, and the compromised component appeared to be the market initializer rather than the contracts themselves.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto identified the affected component as Polymarket\\u2019s UMA CTF Adapter, which connects prediction-market settlement to UMA\\u2019s Optimistic Oracle.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto, citing Bubblemaps, said the exploiter dispersed the stolen proceeds across 15 addresses, a pattern often associated with early-stage laundering attempts.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto added broader context that May had already seen five hacks in the prior week, 19 incidents for the month, and roughly $38.2 million in cumulative exploit losses according to DeFiLlama data.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"# ZachXBT flags $520K Polymarket exploit on Polygon, team says funds are safe\\n\\n## Blockchain investigator ZachXBT has highlighted a suspected security breach involving Polymarket, the world\\u2019s largest decentralized prediction market platform.\\n\\n#### What to know:\\n\\n- Blockchain sleuth ZachXBT flagged a suspected security breach at Polymarket in which more than $520,000 was drained from two smart contracts on the Polygon blockchain.\\n- Polymarket developers said the incident stemmed from a private key compromise of an internal operations wallet tied to its rewards payout system, stressing that user funds and market resolutions remain safe.\\n\\nBlockchain investigator ZachXBT has highlighted a suspected security breach involving Polymarket, the world\\u2019s largest decentralized prediction market platform.\\n\\nOver $520,000 was reportedly drained from two smart contracts on the Polygon blockchain, according to on-chain data shared by ZachXBT. The affected addresses are 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5, with funds allegedly sent to attacker address 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\\n\\nPolymarket developers said the company is aware of reports tied to its rewards payout system in a post on X. The team emphasized that user funds and market resolutions remain safe, describing the issue as a private key compromise of an internal operations wallet rather than a broader smart contract exploit or core infrastructure breach. Further updates are expected.\\n\\nPolygon Labs' CTO Mudit Gupta also commented on the incident, stating:\\n\\n\\u201cPolymarket contracts are safe. User funds are safe. Looks like their market initializer was compromised. No impact to the users or the contracts.\\u201d\\n\\nPolymarket has not yet issued an official statement from its main X account. CoinDesk has reached out to the company for additional comment. The incident comes amid heightened scrutiny of decentralized finance platforms.\\n\\n**On-chain investigator ZachXBT issued a community alert flagging an apparent exploit of Polymarket\\u2019s UMA CTF Adapter.**\\n\\nThe UMA CTF Adapter is the smart contract that lets Polymarket\\u2019s prediction markets settle using UMA\\u2019s Optimistic Oracle\\n\\nAccording to the alert, attackers have drained more than $520,000 so far. ZachXBT highlighted that the attacker\\u2019s address was 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\\n\\nAccording to Bubblemaps, the exploiter has since dispersed the proceeds across 15 addresses. This pattern is typically seen in the early stages of an on-chain laundering attempt.\\n\\n*Follow us on X** to get the latest news as it happens*\\n\\nThe situation is developing, and the full scope of the incident, as well as any response from Polymarket, has yet to be confirmed. BeInCrypto has contacted Polymarket for comment on the reported exploit and will update this story as more information becomes available.\\n\\nThe Polymarket incident lands amid a broader spike in DeFi exploits this month. May has already produced five separate hacks in the past week alone, pushing the monthly tally to 19, according to DeFiLlama data. Cumulative losses across those incidents have reached roughly $38.2 million.\\n\\n*Subscribe to our YouTube** channel to watch leaders and journalists provide expert insights*\\n\\nFeed metadata and corroborating details:\\n- CoinDesk RSS publication time: 2026-05-22T09:39:55+00:00. Headline: ZachXBT flags $520K Polymarket exploit on Polygon, team says funds are safe. Summary: Blockchain investigator ZachXBT has highlighted a suspected security breach involving Polymarket, the world\\u2019s largest decentralized prediction market platform.\\n- BeInCrypto RSS publication time: 2026-05-22T08:32:43+00:00. Headline: ZachXBT Flags Polymarket Smart-Contract Exploit With $520,000 in Losses. Summary: On-chain investigator ZachXBT issued a community alert flagging an apparent exploit of Polymarket\\u2019s UMA CTF Adapter. The UMA CTF Adapter is the smart contract that lets Polymarket\\u2019s prediction markets settle using UMA\\u2019s Optimistic Oracle.\\n- CoinDesk article references two affected addresses: 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5. The alleged attacker address is 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\\n- BeInCrypto reports Bubblemaps said the exploiter dispersed proceeds across 15 addresses, a pattern it described as typical of an early-stage laundering attempt.\\n- CoinDesk embedded/linked X posts: https://x.com/PolymarketDevs/status/2057755280641814769 and https://x.com/Mudit__Gupta/status/2057749582575202570.\\n- URL validation results before inclusion: CoinDesk 200 OK; BeInCrypto returned 403 to direct curl, but its RSS entry and trafilatura extraction still produced article text, title-aligned facts, and publication timestamp.\\n\",\n  \"tweet_quotes\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://x.com/PolymarketDevs/status/2057755280641814769\",\n      \"author\": \"@PolymarketDevs\",\n      \"text\": \"We\\u2019re aware of the security reports linked to rewards payout. User funds and market resolution are safe. \\n\\nFindings point to a private key compromise of a wallet used for internal top-up operations, not contracts or core infrastructure.\\n\\nMore updates to follow.\",\n      \"verified\": true,\n      \"source_article_url\": \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/22/zachxbt-flags-usd520k-polymarket-exploit-on-polygon-team-says-funds-are-safe\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://x.com/Mudit__Gupta/status/2057749582575202570\",\n      \"author\": \"@Mudit__Gupta\",\n      \"text\": \"Polymarket contracts are safe. User funds are safe.\\n\\nLooks like their market initializer was compromised. No impact to the users or the contracts.\",\n      \"verified\": true,\n      \"source_article_url\": \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/22/zachxbt-flags-usd520k-polymarket-exploit-on-polygon-team-says-funds-are-safe\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779445739187}}
{"type":"message","id":"ed90d5c0","parentId":"fa1ce8b7","timestamp":"2026-05-22T10:28:59.188Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_IiYs4njqByR0BKDCBjaCINTO","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL RULES\n\nFollow these rules for every article. Interior structure (how many sections, headings, hook style) is your editorial decision within the borders below.\n\n---\n\n## Hard rules (never break)\n\n**Content quality**\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use `—`. Use a comma, period, or restructure.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype or fluff (\"amazing\", \"shocking\").\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only, inside H3 sections if you use them. Never in Conclusion or opening hook.\n\n**SEO and links**\n- **ANCHOR LINKS:** Exactly **2** markdown links to URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON.source_urls` (distinct URLs, no repeats). Both in the **opening hook or first H2 only**.\n- **TWEET QUOTES (optional):** If `tweet_quotes` has `verified: true`, weave **one** short quote in hook or first H2. Max **1** tweet link (separate from the 2 source anchors).\n- **KEYWORDS:** Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words); in first 100 words; in at least one H2 and in Conclusion. Use secondary keywords in H2/H3 where natural.\n\n**Length**\n- **Word count:** Body **1000–1200** words (aim **1100**). Count body only; META, Sources, and `[Word Count:]` line are not counted.\n\n**Fixed section order**\n```\nMETA → H1 (hook) → H2 body sections → ## Conclusion → ## FAQs → **Sources:** → [Word Count: NNNN]\n```\n- **## Conclusion** must appear immediately before **## FAQs**.\n- **## FAQs** must be the **last** section in the content body (immediately before **Sources:**).\n- Do not put FAQs before Conclusion or interleave FAQs in the body.\n\n---\n\n## Structure borders (you decide inside these limits)\n\n| Element | Minimum | Maximum |\n|---------|---------|---------|\n| H2 body sections (excluding Conclusion, FAQs) | 2 | 4 |\n| H3 subsections (`###`) total | 3 | 6 |\n| FAQ items under `## FAQs` | 3 | 6 |\n\nWithin these borders, you choose:\n- How many H2s, H3s, and FAQs to use\n- Heading text and angle for each section\n- How to open the article (2–4 paragraphs under H1)\n- Section depth, bullet lists, and flow\n\nThere is **no** prescribed mapping from fact count to section count. Decide what structure best serves **this** story.\n\nEach H3 should draw on distinct material from research; do not pad with empty sections.\n\n---\n\n## META block (required first)\n\nThe first word of the file must be **META**.\n\nCount characters in `<thinking>` before writing:\n\n| Field | Your max | Rank Math cap |\n|-------|----------|---------------|\n| SEO Title | **55** | 60 |\n| Meta Description | **155** | 160 |\n| URL Slug | **70** | 75 |\n\n```\nMETA\n- SEO Title: [primary keyword early; add | Coinography only if ≤55 chars total]\n- Meta Description: [hook + detail; ≤155 chars]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase hyphens; 3–5 words; ≤70 chars]\n- Categories: [e.g. Regulation, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [...]\n- Secondary Keywords: [...]\n```\n\n---\n\n## H1 and body\n\nAfter META and `---`, write **one** H1:\n\n`# [Primary keyword in first 5 words; include a price or number when relevant]`\n\nThen your hook and H2/H3 body sections. Heading names and section count are your choice within the borders above.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion (required)\n\n`## Conclusion`\n\nRestate the event with primary keyword and actionable close for traders. Length is your call within the total word band.\n\n---\n\n## FAQs (required, last before Sources)\n\n`## FAQs`\n\nWrite questions that fit **this** story (no fixed question bank). **3–6** items within the FAQ border.\n\nFormat each item:\n```\n**1. Your question here?**\n\nAnswer paragraph, 3–4 sentences.\n```\n\nRenumber 1..N. Do not use markdown list syntax for questions.\n\n---\n\n## Sources footer (required)\n\nDo not use `## Sources` or `## Word Count` as headings.\n\n```\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication]: [URL]\n- [Publication]: [URL]\n\n[Word Count: 1100]\n```\n\nFinal line: `[Word Count: NNNN]` with digits only.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-flight (in `<thinking>`)\n\n- [ ] META char counts within limits\n- [ ] 2 source URLs planned for hook/first H2\n- [ ] Structure choices within H2/H3/FAQ borders\n- [ ] Body word budget 1000–1200\n- [ ] Order: Conclusion then FAQs then Sources\n\n## Post-output check\n\n- [ ] 1 H1; H2/H3/FAQ counts within borders\n- [ ] Conclusion before FAQs; FAQs before Sources\n- [ ] 1000–1200 body words\n- [ ] Exactly 2 source links in hook/first H2\n- [ ] No em-dashes or banned phrases\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779445739188}}
{"type":"message","id":"2c51d9b7","parentId":"ed90d5c0","timestamp":"2026-05-22T10:30:27.786Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_FTC9xZQzD9IzDMcfBXMSRaYj","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-101746/article/raw.md","content":"META\n- SEO Title: Polymarket Exploit Drains $520K on Polygon\n- Meta Description: Polymarket exploit drained over $520K on Polygon. The team said a rewards wallet was hit, while core contracts, user funds, and resolution stayed safe.\n- URL Slug: polymarket-exploit-polygon\n- Categories: Security, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: Polymarket exploit\n- Secondary Keywords: Polygon, UMA CTF Adapter, ZachXBT, prediction markets\n\n---\n\n# Polymarket exploit drains $520K on Polygon\n\nThe Polymarket exploit is in focus after on-chain investigator ZachXBT flagged more than $520,000 drained from two Polygon-linked addresses tied to the platform's rewards flow. According to [CoinDesk](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/22/zachxbt-flags-usd520k-polymarket-exploit-on-polygon-team-says-funds-are-safe) and [BeInCrypto](https://beincrypto.com/polymarket-exploit-with-520000-in-losses/), the incident did not appear to hit the core contracts that hold user positions or control market resolution.\n\nThat distinction matters more than the headline number. For active traders, an exploit tied to an internal wallet or adapter can still damage confidence and create operational noise, but it does not automatically mean open positions, collateral, or settlement logic have been broken.\n\nPolymarket developers said the issue points to a private-key compromise in a wallet used for internal top-up operations for rewards payouts. Polygon Labs CTO Mudit Gupta also said the contracts and user funds were safe, which shifted the market read from systemic contract failure toward access-control failure around a supporting component.\n\n## What happened in the Polymarket exploit\n\nThe first layer of the story is straightforward. ZachXBT said more than $520,000 was drained from two addresses on Polygon, and the reporting tied those losses to Polymarket-related infrastructure rather than a broad protocol collapse.\n\nThe affected addresses reported by CoinDesk were 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5. The funds were allegedly funneled to attacker address 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91, which became the first anchor point for on-chain tracing.\n\n### What on-chain data shows\n\nBeInCrypto said the compromised component was Polymarket's UMA CTF Adapter, the piece that connects prediction market settlement to UMA's Optimistic Oracle. That detail is useful because it narrows the probable blast radius and shows the problem was linked to a settlement support layer, not necessarily to every contract in the stack.\n\nBubblemaps also reportedly observed the stolen funds being spread across 15 addresses. That kind of rapid dispersal does not prove a laundering path by itself, but traders usually read it as a sign that the attacker is trying to complicate recovery and slow direct attribution.\n\n### What the team says was compromised\n\nPolymarket developers did not frame the incident as a failure of market logic. Their public explanation pointed to a private key compromise tied to an internal operations wallet used for rewards payouts, while Gupta said the likely weak point was the market initializer rather than the user-facing contracts.\n\nThose two statements are close enough to support the same working thesis. A supporting wallet or initializer was likely exposed, the exploiter used that access to move funds, and the core engine that tracks markets and user balances remained intact.\n\n## Why user funds still appear ring-fenced\n\nWhen a protocol says user funds are safe after a loss event, traders should always separate message management from verifiable architecture. In this case, the available reporting gives at least some reason to believe the compromise sat outside the contracts that directly hold and settle user exposure.\n\n### Adapter risk versus core contract risk\n\nPrediction market systems often rely on several moving parts. One contract can define markets, another can connect to an oracle, and a separate wallet can handle internal funding, payouts, or upkeep.\n\nIf the compromised piece was the UMA CTF Adapter, the market initializer, or an internal wallet, the event is serious but still different from a vault drain. The market consequence is usually concentrated in operations, confidence, and temporary process risk rather than in an immediate hole in every user's balance.\n\nThat does not make the issue small. A compromised supporting component can still disrupt reward flows, create delays around new market setup, and raise valid questions about key management, privilege boundaries, and whether similar permissions exist elsewhere in the stack.\n\n### What traders should verify next\n\nBefore treating the event as contained, traders should watch for a few specific signals:\n\n- A detailed postmortem that explains which key or contract role was compromised and when access was revoked.\n- Clear confirmation that market settlement, withdrawals, and collateral pathways were tested after the incident.\n- Continued on-chain evidence that no new suspicious outflows are hitting related addresses or linked components.\n\n## What Polygon and prediction market traders should watch now\n\nThe broader context also matters. BeInCrypto placed the Polymarket exploit inside a heavy month for DeFi security incidents, citing 19 exploits in May and roughly $38.2 million in losses across the sector.\n\nThat backdrop changes how the market prices headlines. Even if this incident proves operational rather than systemic, traders have been conditioned to discount reassurance until a team publishes a technical timeline, patches the affected permissions, and shows normal behavior on-chain for a sustained period.\n\n### Near-term signals that matter most\n\nFirst, watch whether Polymarket posts a precise incident report instead of broad reassurance. The more exact the team is about the compromised wallet, adapter, or initializer role, the easier it becomes to separate a one-off key event from a deeper design flaw.\n\nSecond, monitor whether market creation, reward payouts, and resolution continue without anomalies. If open markets settle normally and no user balance issues surface, that supports the view that the Polymarket exploit hit a narrow operational lane.\n\nThird, keep an eye on the attacker's address cluster and any linked bridge activity. Recovery odds often drop quickly once funds are dispersed, but address behavior can still reveal whether this was a targeted internal key compromise, an exploit script against a specific role, or the first sign of a wider campaign against prediction market infrastructure.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe Polymarket exploit looks serious because more than $520,000 was drained, but the current evidence points to a compromised operational path on Polygon rather than a failure of the core contracts that hold user exposure. For traders, the practical move is to ignore both panic and blanket reassurance, then focus on the postmortem, settlement behavior, and any fresh on-chain outflows. If those checks stay clean, the event is more likely to be remembered as a contained access-control breach than as a platform-wide solvency problem.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. Was the Polymarket exploit a direct drain of user funds?**\n\nBased on the reporting so far, no direct user-fund drain has been confirmed. Polymarket developers said user funds and market resolution were safe, and Polygon Labs CTO Mudit Gupta echoed that view. The available evidence points instead to a private-key compromise involving an internal operations wallet or related support component.\n\n**2. What was allegedly compromised in this incident?**\n\nThe reports point to a supporting layer tied to rewards payouts and market operations. BeInCrypto identified the affected piece as the UMA CTF Adapter, while public comments from the team and Gupta referred to an internal top-up wallet or market initializer. Those descriptions differ in wording, but all suggest the compromise sat outside the main user-facing contract balances.\n\n**3. Why are traders paying attention if core contracts seem safe?**\n\nBecause access-control failures still matter even when user balances are untouched. They can expose weak key management, reveal over-privileged roles, and create uncertainty around related components that have not been audited in public after the event. For active traders, that uncertainty can affect sizing, venue trust, and how much weight to place on short-term platform risk.\n\n**4. What should traders watch over the next 24 to 72 hours?**\n\nThe key signals are a full incident report, stable market resolution, and no evidence of fresh suspicious transfers from linked addresses. Traders should also watch whether Polymarket changes permissions, rotates keys, or pauses any reward-related functions while the investigation continues. If the team provides exact technical details and the chain stays quiet, confidence can recover faster.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CoinDesk: https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/22/zachxbt-flags-usd520k-polymarket-exploit-on-polygon-team-says-funds-are-safe\n- BeInCrypto: https://beincrypto.com/polymarket-exploit-with-520000-in-losses/\n\n[Word Count: 0]"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":11604,"output":4246,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":15850,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779445739189,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiHPgqw7VfVOw656Cb9pPTNkqWd2g"}}
